* Privacy at a Glance::          The defaults in one table.
* What Codebud Stores::          Files on disk, who can read them, masking.
* Encryption at Rest::           An age key sealed with your passphrase.
* Where Data Can Go::            Every outbound connection and its switch.
* Choosing How Private::         Privacy levels, isolation, unattended work.
* Checking Privacy::             doctor, config and the local-only test.
* What Privacy Does Not Cover::  The limits, plainly.
@end menu


@node Privacy at a Glance
@section Privacy at a Glance
@cindex privacy defaults

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .66
@headitem Topic @tab Default
@item Prompts and replies
@tab Sent only to the Ollama on this machine (@code{localhost:11434}).
@item Cloud models
@tab Never used unless you choose @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai}; never a
fallback when Ollama is down.
@item Telemetry, update checks
@tab None.  Codebud contacts nothing on its own behalf.
@item Web search
@tab Off; when on, every query is shown and (outside @code{full-auto})
confirmed.
@item Command steps
@tab Isolated: no network, project-only writes, your secrets hidden ---
files and environment variables alike.
@item Stored data
@tab Owner-only files (0700/0600); secrets masked as @samp{[redacted]};
optionally encrypted with your passphrase (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).
@item Retention
@tab Kept until you delete it; deletion lists what it removes and asks.
@item A cloned repository
@tab Cannot change any of the above through its @file{.codebud/config.toml}.
@end multitable


@node What Codebud Stores
@section What Codebud Stores
@cindex stored data

Everything lives in two directories (@pxref{Files and Directories}):

@table @file
@item ~/.local/share/codebud/
(@env{XDG_DATA_HOME}) --- @file{memory/entries.jsonl}, the history of
executed steps with their output; @file{sessions/}, saved chat
conversations; @file{index/}, project indexes with copies of your
files' text (@pxref{The index Subcommand}).
@item ~/.config/codebud/
(@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}) --- your @file{config.toml} and policy files.
@end table

@noindent
Nothing else is written outside your project, except what the steps you
approve do there.

@subheading Readable only by you

Directories are created 0700 and files 0600, whatever your
@code{umask}; @command{codebud doctor} warns if anything in these
directories is readable by other users and gives the @command{chmod}
that fixes it.

@subheading Secrets are masked

Before a session or a step is saved, API keys and tokens, private keys,
passwords in URLs and secret-looking assignments are replaced by
@samp{[redacted]} (@env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS}, on by default).  What is
sent to the model is not changed.  Secret files (@file{.env},
@file{*.pem}, @file{id_rsa}, @dots{}) are never put into a project index.

@subheading Deleting

@example
codebud sessions --delete @var{name}         @r{# one conversation}
codebud sessions --purge [--older-than 30]  @r{# many}
codebud history --purge                     @r{# the step history}
codebud index --drop                        @r{# a project index}
@end example

@noindent
Each lists the files it removes and asks first (@option{--yes} for
scripts).  @code{session_retention_days} in your @file{config.toml}
deletes old sessions automatically (@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).


@node Encryption at Rest
@section Encryption at Rest
@cindex encryption at rest
@cindex age encryption
@cindex passphrase
@cindex key subcommand

Codebud can encrypt the data it keeps with @uref{https://age-encryption.org,
age}, so that a copy of your disk, a backup or another program running as
you cannot read your conversations and step history without your
passphrase.  It is off by default.  It needs the optional @code{pyrage}
module (@pxref{Python Environment}); @command{codebud key status} says
whether it is installed.

@subheading Turning it on

@example
codebud key init                                @r{# once: create the key}
echo 'encrypt = true' >> ~/.config/codebud/config.toml
codebud doctor                                  @r{# Encryption at rest: on}
@end example

@noindent
@env{CODEBUD_ENCRYPT=1} does the same for one shell, and a profile with
@code{encrypt = true} turns it on while it is active (@pxref{Profiles}).
A profile can only turn encryption on, never off, and a project's
@file{.codebud/config.toml} can do neither --- like the other settings
that protect your data, @code{encrypt} is read only from your own file
and the environment.

If encryption is on but cannot work --- no key yet, or @code{pyrage}
missing --- @command{run}, @command{plan}, @command{chat},
@command{history}, @command{sessions} and @command{index} refuse to start
and say how to fix it, rather than store anything unencrypted;
@command{codebud doctor} fails with the same fix.

@subheading What is encrypted

@table @asis
@item Chat sessions
Each session file (@file{sessions/@var{id}.json}) is an age file under
the same name.
@item The step history
Each entry of @file{memory/entries.jsonl} is encrypted on its own.
@item Project indexes
A new index is stored as @file{index.sqlite3.age} (@pxref{The index
Subcommand}).  SQLite cannot work on an encrypted file, so while an index
is in use it is decrypted into a private directory under
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} (@file{/run/user/@var{uid}}: in memory, 0700,
emptied when you log out) and that copy is removed when Codebud is done
with it --- SQLite is told to keep its temporary files for that copy in
memory too, since it would otherwise put them in @file{/var/tmp}, usually
on disk; @command{codebud index} encrypts the updated index back.
Codebud checks the directory first: it must be yours and mode 0700, and
on GNU/Linux really in memory (a @code{tmpfs} or @code{ramfs} file system,
read from @file{/proc/self/mountinfo}).  A copy left behind by a Codebud
that crashed or was killed is removed the next time an index is opened.
Without a suitable @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} (it is set by systemd and most
desktop sessions on GNU/Linux, usually not on macOS or the BSDs) an
encrypted index is not used at all rather than decrypted to disk: project search is
then unavailable, with a message saying why.  An index built before
encryption was on stays a plain @file{index.sqlite3} until
@command{key encrypt-existing} converts it.
@end table

@noindent
Sessions and history saved before you turned encryption on stay as they
were, in plain text, and remain readable; everything written afterwards
is encrypted.  Turning encryption off again leaves encrypted files
readable too (with the passphrase), and new data is then written in plain
text.

@subheading Encrypting what was saved before
@cindex encrypt-existing

@example
codebud key encrypt-existing
@end example

@noindent
lists the plaintext sessions, step history and project indexes and asks
first
(@option{--yes} for scripts; without a terminal it does nothing unless
given @option{--yes}).  For each file it then:

@enumerate
@item copies the original, unchanged, into
@file{~/.local/share/codebud/plaintext-backup-@var{time}/} (same layout,
0600) and checks the copy matches;
@item only then replaces the live file with its encrypted version
(atomically; history lines already encrypted are kept as they are).
@end enumerate

@noindent
Codebud never deletes the backup: it prints where it is, and you remove
it once you have checked that @command{codebud sessions} and
@command{codebud history} read everything back (@command{rm -r} on the
directory it names).  Until then your plaintext is still on disk.
Sessions open in a chat are skipped --- run it again after closing them.
Older plaintext Codebud kept aside (@file{entries.json.migrated},
quarantined @file{*.corrupt-*} files) is listed but not converted.  No
passphrase is needed: encrypting uses only the public key.

Deleting a file does not always remove its contents from the disk
(journaling and copy-on-write file systems, SSDs, backups and snapshots
may keep copies); encryption protects what is written from then on.

@subheading What needs the passphrase

Saving never does: running steps, @command{plan --execute} and every chat
turn write with the public key alone, so an unattended run can store
encrypted data without knowing the passphrase.  Reading does, once per
run: @command{codebud history}, @command{codebud sessions} (listing and
deleting), @command{chat --resume}, naming a session with
@command{/name}, and the automatic retention clean-up when
@code{session_retention_days} is set.  Building a @emph{new} index needs
no passphrase either; refreshing or reading an existing encrypted one
does.

Project context is the one read that happens during planning.  Codebud
never asks for the passphrase in the middle of a plan: when the project
has an encrypted index, @command{run}, @command{plan} and @command{chat}
ask once at startup, before anything else.  If the key cannot be unlocked
there (a wrong passphrase three times, or no terminal and no
@env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE}), they carry on without project context and say
so; the chat toolbar shows @samp{RAG locked}.  @option{--no-rag} skips
the question.

@subheading One key, sealed with your passphrase

@example
codebud key init        @r{# create the key; asks for a passphrase twice}
codebud key status      @r{# where the key is, and its public half}
codebud key passwd      @r{# change the passphrase}
@end example

@command{key init} creates one age key pair in
@file{~/.config/codebud/keys/} (@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}) --- never in the
data directory it protects:

@table @file
@item identity.age
The secret key, itself encrypted with your passphrase.  It is never
written to disk in the clear.
@item recipient.txt
The public key (@samp{age1@dots{}}).  While the key is locked, Codebud
encrypts to it; every unlock checks that it still belongs to your key and
stops with a clear message if it does not (someone replacing it would
otherwise have new data encrypted to their key instead of yours).  While
the key is unlocked, Codebud encrypts to the public key derived from the
key itself.
@end table

@noindent
The directory is 0700 and both files 0600.  A new passphrase must be at
least 12 characters: unlocking is deliberately slow (about a second per
try), but a short passphrase still falls to someone patient who has a
copy of @file{identity.age}; several unrelated words make a good one.
@command{key init} refuses to replace an existing key, since data encrypted to it would become
unreadable.  @command{key passwd} re-seals the same key with a new
passphrase, so everything already encrypted stays readable.

@subheading When the passphrase is needed

Writing encrypted data needs only the public key: Codebud never asks for
the passphrase to save something.  Reading needs the secret key, so the
first read in a run asks for the passphrase on the terminal (three tries)
and keeps the unlocked key in memory until Codebud exits --- one prompt
per run, not one per file.  Unlocking takes about a second: age derives
the key from the passphrase with scrypt, deliberately slowly, to make
guessing expensive.

For unattended runs, the passphrase may come from @env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE}
instead (one try, since nobody is there to retype it).  Codebud takes it
out of its environment as soon as it starts and keeps it only in memory,
so the commands it runs never inherit it --- a step running
@command{printenv} or @command{env} cannot see it.  Still, whatever
started Codebud with it, and anything that can read that environment,
can unlock your data, so @command{codebud doctor} warns while it is set;
prefer the prompt.

@subheading While the key is unlocked

From the moment Codebud holds the passphrase or the unlocked key until it
exits, it does not write core dumps (a crash would otherwise leave the key
and your decrypted data in one, for example under
@file{/var/lib/systemd/coredump}), and on GNU/Linux it marks itself
non-dumpable, which also stops other programs running as you from
attaching to it or reading its memory.

@subheading If you lose the passphrase

The data encrypted to the key cannot be recovered --- not by you, not by
Codebud.  There is no back door and no recovery key.  Keep the passphrase
in a password manager; a copy of @file{identity.age} is only as safe as
the passphrase that seals it.

@subheading Without a terminal

The passphrase is read from the terminal without echo.  Where there is no
terminal (a script, a cron job, a pipe), Codebud does not fall back to
reading standard input --- that would echo it --- but stops with a
message; use @env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE} there, or keep such runs to
commands that only write.

@subheading How the files are encrypted

Whole files are ordinary age files and can be decrypted with the
@command{age} tool and the unsealed key.  Files that are only ever
appended to cannot be appended to as one age file, so each line is
encrypted on its own and stored as @samp{age:} followed by the age file
in base64 --- still one line per entry.


@node Where Data Can Go
@section Where Data Can Go
@cindex outbound connections
@cindex egress

These are all the connections Codebud makes.  @command{codebud doctor}
lists the ones currently enabled on its @samp{Outbound:} line.

@table @asis
@item The Ollama server (@env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL})
Prompts --- your request, the conversation, retrieved project context ---
and text to embed for project search.  By default this is
@code{localhost}, so nothing leaves the machine.  A server on another
machine is announced when a command starts and shown in the chat
toolbar; over plain @code{http} it is refused unless you allow it, since
your code would cross the network unencrypted (@pxref{LLM Backend
Variables}).

@item OpenAI
Only with @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai}: then prompts go to OpenAI, which
Codebud announces when a command starts and marks with @samp{☁ OpenAI}
in the chat toolbar.

@item DuckDuckGo
Only with @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1}: the model's search queries go to
@code{lite.duckduckgo.com}.  Each query is printed, and confirmed before
it is sent except in @code{full-auto} mode (@pxref{web_search}).

@item Steps that declare @code{"network": true}
A command such as @command{pip install} may reach the internet when the
plan says it needs to; the step is shown and confirmed like a web search
(@pxref{Isolated Steps}).  Every other command step has no network.
@end table

@noindent
Outside Codebud: @command{ollama pull} downloads models from Ollama's
registry, and Ollama itself is configured separately; neither sends your
prompts anywhere.

A project's @file{.codebud/config.toml} cannot set any of these switches
(the backend, the Ollama URL, plain-http opt-in, the OpenAI key, web
search), nor the privacy level, secret masking or retention: cloning a
repository must never redirect your data or weaken your privacy.


@node Choosing How Private
@section Choosing How Private
@cindex privacy levels, overview

How much Codebud asks (the mode, @pxref{Agent Modes}) and what it may
reach (the privacy level) are separate dials:

@table @code
@item locked
Nothing leaves this machine: a backend that is not local, web search and
network steps are refused, and command steps must be isolated or they do
not run.
@item private
The default: everything above.
@item trusted
For trusted environments: command steps run unisolated, and web searches
and network steps run without asking.
@end table

@noindent
Choose with @option{--privacy}, @code{/privacy} in @command{chat}, or
@code{privacy =} in your @file{config.toml} (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).
@strong{No level lifts the safety rules}: the allow-list, the refused
arguments and the confirmation of powerful commands always apply.

Fully unattended work is always possible: @code{full-auto} mode asks
nothing, at the default level too; @code{trusted} additionally drops
isolation.  The built-in profile @code{unattended} sets both
(@option{--profile unattended}, @pxref{Profiles}).  The level in effect is announced, shown in the toolbar and
recorded in the session file; a resumed session never silently becomes
@code{trusted}.


@node Checking Privacy
@section Checking Privacy
@cindex privacy, checking

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

@noindent
reports, among the rest:

@example
  [PASS] LLM backend: ollama at http://localhost:11434 (local)
  [PASS] Privacy: private (data leaves only where you chose)
  [PASS] Isolation: bubblewrap: no network, project-only writes, secrets hidden
  [PASS] Outbound: none - prompts, code and searches stay on this machine
  [PASS] Data and config files are private (owner only)
@end example

@command{codebud config} shows each setting and where it came from
(environment, project or user @file{config.toml}, default).  The test
suite includes @file{tests/test_local_only.py}, which refuses every
connection to an address that is not this machine and runs planning,
execution, indexing and search against a local Ollama --- with an OpenAI
key present --- so a change that reached outward would fail it.


@node What Privacy Does Not Cover
@section What Privacy Does Not Cover
@cindex privacy, limits

@itemize
@item
@strong{What you send is what the backend sees.}  With a remote Ollama or
the OpenAI backend, your requests, conversation and retrieved project
context go there; their privacy is that server's.

@item
@strong{File tools are confined, not sandboxed.}  @code{file_read},
@code{file_write} and @code{patch} run inside Codebud, with your rights;
they may write only inside the project (not its @file{.git/} or
@file{.codebud/}) and never read your secret files or Codebud's own
(@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).

@item
@strong{Isolation needs bubblewrap and user namespaces.}  On macOS, the
BSDs, in many containers and where user namespaces are blocked, command
steps are not isolated (a warning at @code{private}, refused at
@code{locked}).

@item
@strong{Masking is pattern-based.}  It catches common token formats,
private keys and secret-looking assignments (@pxref{Files and
Directories} for the list), not every secret: a secret with no
recognisable format or name, or shorter than 8 characters, is stored as
it is.  The project index keeps your files' text as it is.

@item
@strong{Encryption at rest covers what Codebud stores, not what it is
doing.}  While Codebud runs, your data is in its memory and the unlocked
key with it, and an index in use has its decrypted copy in
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} --- memory, not disk, unless the system swaps it
out: use encrypted swap (or none) if that matters to you.  Python cannot
wipe the key from memory when it is done with it.  What your approved
steps write into your project is not Codebud's to encrypt.  Isolated
command steps cannot read the key or a decrypted index copy: isolation
hides Codebud's configuration, data and runtime directories from them
(@pxref{Isolated Steps}), and their environment never holds the
passphrase.  Steps that are not isolated (privacy @code{trusted}, or no
bubblewrap) run with your rights and could read whatever you can.

@item
@strong{Some facts about your data stay visible.}  File names, sizes and
times are not encrypted: a session's name on disk is the time it was
started, and a project index's directory is named after a hash of the
project's path --- not secret enough to stop someone who guesses the
path from confirming it.

@item
@strong{Changing the passphrase is not changing the key.}
@command{key passwd} re-seals the same key, so an old copy of
@file{identity.age} (a backup, say) still opens with the old passphrase.
If you think the key itself is exposed, the remedy today is a new key
and @command{key encrypt-existing} on plaintext copies of your data;
replacing the key in place is not built yet.

@item
@strong{Not post-quantum yet.}  The key is an X25519 key, the age
standard.  age 1.3 adds hybrid post-quantum keys, but @code{pyrage} does
not offer them yet; Codebud will move to them when it does.

@item
@strong{Your terminal and Ollama are your own.}  Scrollback, shell
history and Ollama's logs are outside Codebud's control.
@end itemize
